<article>
  <h1>Cross-Sectional Study</h1>

  <p>
    Researchers concluded that anxiety among young adults increased between 2014 and 2023, based on the increase in the percentage of 18-25 year olds reporting frequent anxiety symptoms from 8.1% in 2014 to 14.7% in 2023.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each year, researchers drew a new random sample of 27,000 adults. Participants were surveyed only once, and the same individuals were not followed over time. Each year's sample was independent from the previous years.
  </p>

  <p>
    This claim is not fully supported by the study design. Because different people were surveyed each year, the study cannot determine whether the same individuals became more anxious throughout their twenties. The design assumes that changes observed between yearly samples reflect changes within individuals, but that relationship was never established. Additionally, the change in survey mode from in-person interviews to a mix of phone and web interviews could have affected responses.
  </p>

  <p>
    More broadly, cross-sectional studies can identify patterns and differences between groups or time points, but they cannot determine individual change over time. Cross-sectional data can support descriptions of associations and trends within samples, but they cannot establish whether the same people experienced those changes.
  </p>

  <p>
    A more accurate representation of the findings would be: The proportion of adults aged 18-25 reporting anxiety symptoms increased substantially between 2014 and 2023. Because the survey used independent samples each year, the data cannot determine whether the same group developed more anxiety over time.
  </p>
</article>
